          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which single risk factor carries the highest relative increase in risk for developmental dysplasia of the hip?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Female sex</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Female sex raises the risk sixfold due to maternal oestrogen-mediated ligament laxity — significant, but not the strongest single risk factor discussed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Breech presentation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Breech presentation raises DDH risk twentyfold by restricting hip movement in utero, preventing normal acetabular modelling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Affected first-degree relative</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A positive family history raises the risk tenfold — substantial, but breech presentation (20×) carries a higher relative risk.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reduced amniotic fluid</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Oligohydramnios is a recognised risk factor by restricting foetal movement, but its relative risk is not quantified as highly as breech (20×), family history (10×), or female sex (6×).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Breech presentation carries a twentyfold increase in DDH risk — the highest among the factors discussed. Female sex (6×) and positive family history (10×) are also significant but lower in magnitude."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Breech presentation carries a twentyfold risk increase — the highest of any single factor — because it severely restricts hip movement during the critical window of acetabular development."
                }
              ],
