          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which component of the CTEV deformity results from contracture of the Achilles t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which component of the CTEV deformity results from contracture of the Achilles tendon and the posterior ankle joint capsule?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cavus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cavus is the abnormally high longitudinal arch caused by the forefoot flexing plantarward relative to the hindfoot — it is not related to the Achilles tendon.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adductus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adductus describes the inward deviation of the forefoot and midfoot, resulting from medial soft tissue contractures rather than Achilles tendon tightness.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Varus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Varus refers to the inward rotation of the calcaneus beneath the talus — a hindfoot alignment problem, not a direct consequence of Achilles contracture.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Equinus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Equinus — the downward, horse-hoof-like position — results directly from a tight Achilles tendon and posterior capsule preventing dorsiflexion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Equinus — the plantar-flexed, downward-pointing position — is caused specifically by contracture of the Achilles tendon and posterior ankle capsule, distinguishing it from cavus (high arch), adductus (forefoot deviation), and varus (heel inversion)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Equinus is the last component corrected during Ponseti serial casting; the tight Achilles tendon is typically released by percutaneous tenotomy only after cavus, adductus, and varus have been addressed."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
