          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 3-month-old infant is referred with a suspected unstable left hip. Which is th</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 3-month-old infant is referred with a suspected unstable left hip. Which is the most appropriate initial investigation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anteroposterior pelvic radiograph</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Before 4–6 months of age, the femoral head is entirely cartilaginous and invisible on plain X-ray. AP radiography becomes useful only after the ossification centre appears.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ultrasound of both hips</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! At 3 months the femoral head is still cartilaginous. Ultrasound visualises it directly, measures the Graf alpha and beta angles, and assesses hip stability in real time.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>MRI of the pelvis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MRI is reserved for pre-operative planning — for example, identifying soft tissue obstacles when closed reduction fails — not for initial screening in a young infant.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CT scan with 3D reconstruction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CT carries significant radiation and offers no advantage over ultrasound for evaluating a cartilaginous femoral head. It has no role in initial DDH screening.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "At 3 months the femoral head has not yet ossified, making X-ray and CT uninformative. Ultrasound is the investigation of choice, using the Graf classification to measure the bony roof (alpha) and cartilaginous roof (beta) angles."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The rule is straightforward: ultrasound before 4–6 months while the femoral head is cartilaginous, then AP pelvic radiograph once ossification begins and bony landmarks become visible."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
